"03_kernel.mst — the compat kernel classes (DolphinDart DD8).

 The bridge classes Dolphin's MVP corpus assumes but this VM's world does not
 provide, per the prior-art G3 inventory. Everything here is small and
 hand-written; anything large enough to be worth translating is not here.

 NAMING RULE, learned four times over in this sprint: before naming a compat
 method, check the selector is not a UNIVERSAL HELPER. The IL builder rewrites
 selectors like `at:`, `at:put:`, `size`, `asString`, `valueWithArguments:` AT
 THE CALL SITE, so a method of that name is never reached — the send lands
 elsewhere, often on a class value, and surfaces as `_Type has no instance
 method …`. See docs/HOUSE_PRIMS.md."

"── Model / ValueModel are NOT here any more (DD10) ────────────────────
 They were DD8 stand-ins, written so the compat layer had a model side at all
 before any of the corpus was translated. DD10 translates Dolphin's own
 `Core.Model` and `UI.ValueModel` into `st/mvp`, and keeping both would mean
 two classes of each name with the winner decided by load order — the exact
 silent-wrong-answer shape this project keeps paying for. Their contracts are
 now asserted against Dolphin's own classes in `test/st_triad.dart`."

"── SearchPolicy: how a collection compares its elements ────────────────
 Dolphin parameterises Set/Dictionary lookup with one of these. v1 provides the
 two the corpus uses; anything needing a custom policy can subclass."
Object subclass: SearchPolicy [
    SearchPolicy class >> equality [ ^EqualitySearchPolicy new ]
    SearchPolicy class >> identity [ ^IdentitySearchPolicy new ]
    compare: a with: b [ ^self subclassResponsibility ]
    hashOf: anObject [ ^self subclassResponsibility ]

    "A policy is also a FACTORY for collections that obey it. Dolphin's model
     classes never send `Dictionary new` — they send `searchPolicy
     newLookupTable`, so the collection and the comparison it will use are
     chosen together and cannot disagree. `TreeModel>>initialize` is one
     (`objectNodeMap := searchPolicy newLookupTable`) and
     `TreeView>>initialize` another, so a policy without this is not a missing
     convenience: `TreeView new` cannot complete."
    newLookupTable [ ^self subclassResponsibility ]
    newSet [ ^self subclassResponsibility ]
]
SearchPolicy subclass: EqualitySearchPolicy [
    compare: a with: b [ ^a = b ]
    hashOf: anObject [ ^anObject hash ]
    newLookupTable [ ^LookupTable new ]
    newSet [ ^Set new ]
]
SearchPolicy subclass: IdentitySearchPolicy [
    compare: a with: b [ ^a == b ]
    hashOf: anObject [ ^anObject identityHash ]
    newLookupTable [ ^IdentityDictionary new ]
    newSet [ ^IdentitySet new ]
]

"── DeafObject: swallows everything ─────────────────────────────────────
 Dolphin uses it as a null-object for torn-down views: sends land and do
 nothing rather than raising. Built on doesNotUnderstand:, which this VM
 reaches for ST receivers (DD4)."
Object subclass: DeafObject [
    <classVars: Current>
    DeafObject class >> current [
        Current isNil ifTrue: [ Current := self basicNew ]. ^Current
    ]
    "Read as `DeafObject.Current` in the corpus (12 sites), which the
     translator rewrites to this accessor."
    DeafObject class >> classVarCurrent [ ^self current ]
    doesNotUnderstand: aMessage [ ^nil ]
    isNil [ ^false ]
    notNil [ ^true ]
]

"── GUID: `GUID newUnique` is all the corpus needs ──────────────────────
 Real uniqueness comes from CoCreateGuid; v1 composes one from the clock and a
 counter, which is enough for the registry keys Dolphin uses it for and needs
 no COM. Recorded as a v1 shortcut, not a hidden approximation."
Object subclass: GUID [
    <classVars: Counter>
    | text |
    GUID class >> newUnique [
        Counter := (Counter ifNil: [ 0 ]) + 1.
        ^self basicNew setText:
            '{', Smalltalk millisecondClock printString, '-',
            Counter printString, '}'
    ]
    setText: t [ text := t ]
    printOn: aStream [ aStream nextPutAll: text ]
    = other [ ^(other isKindOf: GUID) and: [ other asText = text ] ]
    hash [ ^text hash ]
    asText [ ^text ]
]

"── property bags ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 Dolphin hangs arbitrary named properties off any object. Same registry shape
 as the event system, and the same v1 strong-reference decision."
Object subclass: PropertyRegistry [
    <classVars: Table>
    PropertyRegistry class >> table [
        Table isNil ifTrue: [ Table := IdentityDictionary new ]. ^Table
    ]
    PropertyRegistry class >> propertiesOf: anObject [
        | p |
        p := self table at: anObject ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        p isNil ifTrue: [ p := Dictionary new. self table at: anObject put: p ].
        ^p
    ]
    PropertyRegistry class >> reset [ Table := IdentityDictionary new ]
]

nil subclass: Object [
    "Dolphin's Object>>initialize — quoting Core.Object.cls: the default is to
     do nothing, and it ANSWERS THE RECEIVER. Both halves are load-bearing.
     `Foo class >> new [ ^super new initialize ]` is the universal constructor
     (147 sites in the corpus), so without this every translated class raised
     `does not understand initialize` the moment DD9 made `super new` allocate;
     and answering anything but self would make that `^` return the wrong
     object. Its instance-side twin, `super initialize` from a subclass's own
     initialize, needs it for the same reason — 8 more sites."
    initialize [ ^self ]

    "ANSI `species`: the class to use when deriving a new object of the same
     kind — `copyFrom:to:`, `collect:`, and a WriteStream growing its backing
     collection all go through it. Absent from the world; found when Dolphin's
     `LayoutContext>>repositionSubViewsOf:` streamed into an Array and the
     stream tried to grow."
    species [ ^self class ]

    "Dolphin marks an object immutable so the VM traps writes to it — a
     primitive this VM has no equivalent for. Answering SELF is the honest
     no-op: the object is genuinely not protected, and pretending otherwise
     (by copying, say) would change identity for something the caller expects
     to be the same object. `View class >> initialize` marks its MessageMap
     this way, which is where it was found.

     Recorded as a KNOWN DIVERGENCE rather than a stub: nothing in the port
     depends on write-trapping, and if something ever does it must be given a
     real mechanism, not this."
    "Dolphin's ExternalHandle protocol, which a raw handle Integer answers
     here because this port passes handles as integers. `View>>
     basicCreateWindow:` tests `handle isNull` right after CreateWindowExW."
    isNull [ ^self = 0 ]
    notNull [ ^self isNull not ]

    beImmutableObject [ ^self ]
    isImmutable [ ^false ]

    "Dolphin's `whileMutableDo:` un-freezes an immutable object for the extent
     of a block and re-freezes it after. Nothing here is ever frozen — see
     `beImmutableObject` above — so this is exactly `aBlock value`, and it is
     the PAIR of that divergence rather than a separate one. `View class >>
     registerMessageMappings:` is the caller: it edits the MessageMap that
     `View class >> initialize` had marked immutable."
    whileMutableDo: aNiladicBlock [ ^aNiladicBlock value ]

    "Dolphin's `release` — drop references to external resources and to other
     objects, so a discarded object does not hold a graph alive. The default
     genuinely does nothing in Dolphin too; subclasses override it. `View>>
     destroy` sends it."
    release [ ^self ]

    "Dolphin's `free` — relinquish an external resource. The DEFAULT does
     nothing, which is what makes `combinedAcceleratorTable free` safe in
     `ShellView>>releaseAccelerators` when no accelerator table was ever made.
     `ExternalMemory>>free` overrides it and really frees.

     nil needs it as much as any object: `nil species` already answers
     UndefinedObject here, so nil does reach Object's protocol — `free` simply
     was not part of it, and the send fell through to Dart's null."
    free [ ^self ]

    "THE NILADIC VALUABLE PROTOCOL — `Object>>value ^self` in Dolphin's
     Core.Object — is NOT here, and must not be. `value` is a universal helper
     (see the naming rule at the top of this file): the builder rewrites it at
     the call site to `stValue0`, so a method of that name is never reached by
     a normal send and, worse, makes `stValue0`'s own slow path resolve back
     into itself. Written here it took out every MVP gate with an access
     violation on a blown stack.

     It lives in `_stValue0Slow` (port-win/dart_st/cocoa.dart) instead, which
     is the single point every niladic-valuable send funnels through — so both
     ST objects and Dart primitives get Dolphin's semantics. It is what makes
     `at: k ifAbsent: 0` legal, and the corpus takes that freedom:
     `UI.View>>getNoRedrawCount` is `^self propertyAt: #_noRedrawCount
     ifAbsent: 0`."

    propertyAt: aKey [ ^(PropertyRegistry propertiesOf: self) at: aKey ifAbsent: [ nil ] ]
    propertyAt: aKey ifAbsent: aBlock [
        ^(PropertyRegistry propertiesOf: self) at: aKey ifAbsent: aBlock
    ]
    propertyAt: aKey put: aValue [
        ^(PropertyRegistry propertiesOf: self) at: aKey put: aValue
    ]

    "REMOVAL completes the protocol. Dolphin's property bag is the storage for
     transient per-object state, and the pattern is always set-then-remove:
     `View>>noRedrawDo:` sets `#_noRedrawCount` on entry and removes it when
     the count returns to zero, so a view that never removes leaks a property
     per redraw suppression. Every list and tree control goes through it on
     creation, via `refreshContents`."
    removePropertyAt: aKey [
        ^(PropertyRegistry propertiesOf: self) removeKey: aKey
    ]
    removePropertyAt: aKey ifAbsent: aBlock [
        ^(PropertyRegistry propertiesOf: self) removeKey: aKey ifAbsent: aBlock
    ]
    hasProperty: aKey [
        ^(PropertyRegistry propertiesOf: self) includesKey: aKey
    ]

    "Dolphin's `expandMacros` fills %1..%9 from an argument array. The corpus
     uses it for status text and dialog captions."
    expandMacrosWith: anArray [
        | out |
        out := self.
        1 to: (anArray size min: 9) do: [ :i |
            out := out copyReplaceAll: '%', i printString
                        with: (anArray at: i) displayString ].
        ^out
    ]
    expandMacrosWith: a with: b [ ^self expandMacrosWith: (Array with: a with: b) ]
    expandMacros [ ^self ]
]

"── exception compat, on DD4's hierarchy ────────────────────────────────
 Dolphin's own error classes. They subclass our Error (DD4), so every existing
 `on: Error do:` still catches them.

 `InvalidFormat` is the one DD10's gate uses in anger: a type converter that
 cannot parse its input signals it, and the presenter beeps and reverts."
Error subclass: Win32Error [
    | code |
    Win32Error class >> signalLastError [
        ^self new setCode: Win32 lastError; signalWithCode
    ]
    setCode: c [ code := c ]
    code [ ^code ]
    signalWithCode [ ^self signal: 'Win32 error ', code printString ]
]
"Dolphin's pre-ANSI `Signal`: a named, raisable condition created with
 `Signal description: 'text'` and raised with `signal`/`signal:`. Kept as a
 real Error subclass so `on: Error do:` catches one, and so the description a
 caller gave it survives to the messageText. `View class >> initialize` builds
 `ViewClosedError := Signal description: 'View closed'` — the first caller."
Error subclass: Signal [
    | description |
    Signal class >> description: aString [
        ^self basicNew setDescription: aString
    ]
    setDescription: aString [ description := aString. messageText := aString ]
    description [ ^description ]
    "Raising a Signal INSTANCE: Dolphin signals the condition object itself,
     so this answers a fresh raise carrying the same description rather than
     mutating the shared condition."
    signalWith: aString [ ^self class new setDescription: aString; signal ]
]

Error subclass: InvalidFormat [ ]
Error subclass: OperationAborted [ ]

"── Character>>asUnicodeValue is NOT here ──────────────────────────────
 It is a SELECTOR difference, not a missing class, so the translator rewrites
 it (tools/dolphin2mst/emit.py, `rewrite_selectors`) exactly as it rewrites
 `??` and `##()`. Reopening `Character` from this file does not work anyway: a
 character literal is a native StChar whose ST protocol lives on the bridged
 `Character ext` holder, and a cross-file reopen of a bridged class does not
 reach it (measured — `$A asUnicodeValue` stayed a doesNotUnderstand under both
 `nil subclass:` and `Magnitude subclass:`).

 The general rule: when Dolphin and the house dialect differ only in SPELLING,
 rewrite at ingestion. Compat classes are for missing BEHAVIOUR."

"── collection protocol Dolphin's own code reaches for (DD10) ──────────────

 Three methods, each found by a translated class failing on it, each Dolphin's
 own definition rather than an invention."
HashedCollection subclass: Dictionary [
    "`add:` an ASSOCIATION. Dolphin's `Collection class >> withAll:` is
     `^self new addAll: aCollection; yourself` and `addAll:` is `do: [:e |
     self add: e]`, so `IdentityDictionary withAll: { #left -> 0. ... }` — the
     shape `TextEdit class >> initialize` uses to build its AlignmentMap —
     reaches `Dictionary>>add:` with an Association. Answering the association
     (not the value) is Dolphin's contract for `add:`."
    add: anAssociation [
        self at: anAssociation key put: anAssociation value.
        ^anAssociation
    ]
]

Object subclass: SequenceableCollection [
    "Dolphin's `pairsDo:`, verbatim:

         1 to: self size by: 2 do: [ :i |
             aDyadicValuable value: (self at: i) value: (self at: i + 1) ]

     `SystemMetrics class >> initialize` builds its SysParamVariables table
     from a flat brace array read two at a time."
    pairsDo: aDyadicValuable [
        1 to: self size by: 2 do: [ :i |
            aDyadicValuable value: (self at: i) value: (self at: i + 1) ]
    ]
]

"`Behavior>>bindingFor:` — SCAFFOLDING, with its cost stated.

 Dolphin answers the live BINDING (an Association) of a class variable, so a
 holder can write through it later. This dialect binds class variables
 statically and has no dynamic binding object, so this answers a detached
 Association instead.

 WHAT THAT COSTS, exactly. The one caller is
 `SystemMetrics class >> initialize`, which builds

     sysParamVars at: setting put: (self bindingFor: varName)

 so that `reset` and `onSettingChanged:` can null those four caches
 (`HasFlatMenus`, `MessageDuration`, `MouseHoverTime`, `WheelScrollLines`)
 when Windows reports a system-setting change. Writing through a detached
 association does not clear the real class variable, so those four values
 stay as first read. A user changing e.g. mouse hover time mid-session would
 not see it until restart. Nothing in the DD10 gates depends on it, and it is
 recorded here rather than silently accepted.

 RETIREMENT: a real binding needs a dynamic class-variable accessor in the
 front-end (`classVarAt:put:`). Worth doing when something needs live setting
 changes — DD12's dialogs do not.

 WHERE IT IS DEFINED, and why on both sides. `SystemMetrics class >>
 initialize` sends `self bindingFor: varName` with `self` being the CLASS, and
 a `self` send inside a class-side method resolves through CLASS-side lookup —
 which does not fall back to `Behavior`'s instance protocol. So the class-side
 definition on `Object` is the one that call actually reaches; the `Behavior`
 instance method is there for a direct send to a class from outside
 (`Object bindingFor: 'x'`), which takes the other path."
nil subclass: Object [
    Object class >> bindingFor: aString [ ^aString -> nil ]
    bindingFor: aString [ ^aString -> nil ]

    "`asExternalHandle` — identity here.

     Dolphin boxes a raw handle in an `ExternalHandle` so that a 64-bit handle
     survives in an image whose SmallIntegers are 30-bit. This port's integers
     are Dart integers, which hold a full 64-bit handle without boxing, so the
     wrapper has nothing to add and `View>>attachHandle:` — `self handle:
     anIntegerOrHandle asExternalHandle` — wants the value straight through.

     On OBJECT rather than Integer, because the same method also has to accept
     something that is already a handle: Dolphin's own `ExternalHandle` answers
     itself here, and the selector is sent to whatever the creation path
     produced."
    asExternalHandle [ ^self ]

    "`asExternalAddress` — identity, for the same reason as
     `asExternalHandle` above. Dolphin boxes a raw address so a 64-bit pointer
     survives its 30-bit SmallIntegers; a Dart integer holds it directly.

     `View>>wmWindowPosChanged:wParam:lParam:` is the caller — it wraps
     lParam, which is a pointer to a WINDOWPOS, before reading fields through
     it. Those two messages arrive on every move and resize, so without this
     every one of them raised into the contained handler-error path.

     It answers an ExternalMemory VIEW, not the integer itself: the receiver
     of `asExternalAddress` is then sent struct protocol (`uint32AtOffset:`),
     which an integer does not have. Answering self looked simpler and moved
     the failure one message along rather than fixing it."
    asExternalAddress [ ^ExternalMemory fromAddress: self ]
]

"`identityIncludes:` — ANSI, and absent from the world.

 Membership by IDENTITY rather than equality. `CommandPolicy>>appendTarget:`
 and `includesTarget:` both use it to avoid putting the same command target on
 the route twice, and `=` would be wrong there: two different views can be
 equal without being the same target."
Object subclass: Collection [
    identityIncludes: anObject [
        self do: [ :each | each == anObject ifTrue: [ ^true ] ].
        ^false
    ]
]

"`conformsToProtocol:` — a BOUNDED stand-in, and the boundary is stated.

 Dolphin registers named protocols on classes and asks
 `aClass conformsToProtocol: #commandTarget` to decide whether an object may
 be put on a command route. There are two senders, both in the command
 framework:

     CommandPolicy>>appendPresenter:   (model class conformsToProtocol: #commandTarget)
                                           ifTrue: [self appendTarget: model]
     CommandDescription>>...           (self command class conformsToProtocol: #commandTarget)

 This port has no protocol registry, and cannot fake one usefully: the
 question is whether INSTANCES of the class understand the protocol, and
 `respondsTo:` (prim 246) answers for the RECEIVER — sent to a class it tests
 the class side, which is the wrong dictionary.

 ANSWERING FALSE is therefore the honest answer, and it is also the common
 one: conforming to `#commandTarget` is opt-in in Dolphin, and ordinary models
 (a ValueHolder, an application object) do not. THE BOUNDARY: a model that
 DOES handle its own commands will not be placed on the command route, so its
 `queryCommand:`/`performCommand:` are never consulted. Commands still route
 through the presenter and view chain, which is where DD10's app puts them.

 RETIREMENT: a real answer needs class-side introspection of the INSTANCE
 method dictionary (`Behavior>>canUnderstand:`), which the front-end does not
 expose. Worth adding with the DD11 reflection work; until then a model that
 needs to handle commands must be given a presenter that does."
nil subclass: Object [
    Object class >> conformsToProtocol: aSymbol [ ^false ]
    conformsToProtocol: aSymbol [ ^false ]
]

"`lookup:` on a SEQUENCEABLE collection — Dolphin's own, and a second meaning.

     ^self at: keyInteger ifAbsent: nil

 `Dictionary>>lookup:` already exists (st/dolphin_compat/06_winview.mst); this
 is the INDEXED twin, and Dolphin defines both for the same reason — it is the
 very common pattern of passing an empty absent-block. `Menu>>fromString:`
 does `label := anArray lookup: 1` on the result of splitting a menu string,
 where a one-part string legitimately has no second element.

 `at: k ifAbsent: nil` — a nil where a block is expected — is Dolphin's own
 spelling and works because `ifAbsent:` sends `value` to whatever it is
 given; written out here as an explicit block, which is the same answer
 without depending on `nil value`."
Object subclass: SequenceableCollection [
    lookup: anIndex [
        ^(anIndex isNil or: [ anIndex < 1 or: [ anIndex > self size ] ])
            ifTrue: [ nil ]
            ifFalse: [ self at: anIndex ]
    ]
    copyFrom: start [
        "The one-argument copyFrom:, i.e. `copyFrom: start to: self size`. An
         out-of-range start answers EMPTY rather than erroring — oracle:
         `(OrderedCollection withAll: #(1 2 3)) copyFrom: 4` -> (). The STx
         filers use it as `readMap copyFrom: self lastPredefined + 1` to drop
         the pre-registered slots, and that start is one past the end whenever
         nothing else has been read yet."
        ^start > self size
            ifTrue: [ self copyFrom: 1 to: 0 ]
            ifFalse: [ self copyFrom: start to: self size ]
    ]
    addAnsweringIndex: anObject [
        "Append anObject, answering its 1-based INDEX rather than the object —
         Dolphin's own idiom for building an identity map you later index into
         (oracle: adding to a 2-element collection answers 3). `STxInFiler>>
         register:` uses it so a deserialised object can be fixed up later by
         the reference number the stream carries."
        self addLast: anObject.
        ^self size
    ]
]

"Two more from Dolphin's base, both reached the first time `MenuBar>>
 fromStrings:` runs.

 `from:do:` — iterate from an index to the end. Dolphin defines it as
 `self from: anInteger keysAndValuesDo: [:i :each | operation value: each]`;
 written directly here, which is the same traversal without needing the
 keys-and-values twin as well. `Menu class >> fromStrings:` uses it to walk a
 nested menu array past its first element, the first element being the menu's
 own title.

 `beFinalizable` — Dolphin marks an object as needing finalization by setting
 a bit its VM reads at collection time. This VM has no finalization hook, so
 this is an honest no-op of exactly the same kind as `beImmutableObject`
 above, and is recorded in `docs/LOOSE_ENDS.md` beside it: a `Menu` that is
 garbage collected will NOT have `DestroyMenu` called for it. That leaks a
 menu handle per discarded menu — bounded, since menus are built once per
 window here, and real if something ever builds them in a loop."
Object subclass: SequenceableCollection [
    from: anInteger do: aBlock [
        anInteger to: self size do: [ :i | aBlock value: (self at: i) ]
    ]
    from: startInteger to: stopInteger do: aBlock [
        startInteger to: stopInteger do: [ :i | aBlock value: (self at: i) ]
    ]
]

nil subclass: Object [
    beFinalizable [ ^self ]
    beUnfinalizable [ ^self ]
]

"`LookupTable` — Dolphin's plain equality-keyed dictionary.

 Dolphin distinguishes `LookupTable` (equality keys, no associations stored)
 from `Dictionary` (stores Associations). Nothing in this port depends on that
 difference — `MenuItem class >> dividers` builds one and only ever reads it
 with `lookup:` — so it is a Dictionary subclass here, which gives it the
 whole protocol for free.

 Recorded rather than aliased outright: if something ever iterates a
 LookupTable expecting VALUES where a Dictionary yields Associations, this is
 the line that made it possible."
Dictionary subclass: LookupTable [
]

"`Sound` — Dolphin's parping, the part the MVP layer reaches.

 `Menu>>applyFlags` ends `ifFalse: [Sound errorBeep]`, and DD10's acceptance
 path wants the bad-input beep. MB_ICONERROR is 16r10, and MessageBeep is
 already in the generated floor.

 Dolphin's `Core.Sound` also plays waveforms from resources; nothing here
 needs that, so it is not written. This is the whole of what the view layer
 asks for, not a stub of something larger."
Object subclass: Sound [
    Sound class >> beep: anInteger [ ^User32 messageBeep: anInteger ]
    Sound class >> errorBeep [ ^self beep: 16r10 ]
    Sound class >> beep [ ^self beep: 0 ]
]

"── the weak-keyed command registry — a STRONG stand-in, cost stated ────────

 `CommandDescription class >> initialize` is

     CommandDescriptionRegistry := WeakIdentityDictionary newWithWeakKeys: 150.
     CurrentId := 4000

 and every `CommandDescription` interns itself there to get a numeric id that
 Windows can carry in a WM_COMMAND. Dolphin makes the KEYS WEAK so a command
 belonging to a discarded menu can be collected and its id reused.

 This VM has no weak references (DIALECT_GAPS A7: weak refs and mourning are
 backlog), so this is an IdentityDictionary with strong keys.

 THE COST, exactly: a CommandDescription is never collected, so its id is
 never reused. Ids start at 4000 and increment. An application that builds
 menus once per window leaks a handful; one that rebuilds a menu in a loop
 grows the registry without bound. Nothing in DD10-DD12 does that, and the
 day something does, the symptom will be memory growth rather than a wrong
 answer — which is why this is acceptable and recorded rather than blocking.

 RETIREMENT: real weak references in the VM. Recorded in docs/LOOSE_ENDS.md.

 `at:ifAbsentPutClean:` is Dolphin's name for at:ifAbsentPut: on a weak table
 — the `Clean` means the block must not capture the receiver, which matters
 only when the table is weak. Here it is exactly at:ifAbsentPut:."
Dictionary subclass: WeakIdentityDictionary [
    WeakIdentityDictionary class >> newWithWeakKeys: anInteger [ ^self new ]
    haveStrongValues [ ^self ]
    haveWeakKeys [ ^self ]
]

Object subclass: Dictionary [
    at: aKey ifAbsentPutClean: aBlock [ ^self at: aKey ifAbsentPut: aBlock ]
]

"`FunctionDescriptor` — a stand-in for Dolphin's CALLBACK descriptors.

 `Menu class >> initialize` ends

     DrawStateCallbackDescriptor := FunctionDescriptor
         returnType: 'bool' argumentTypes: 'handle intptr uintptr int32 int32'

 which describes a C callback Windows will invoke for an OWNER-DRAWN menu
 item. Dolphin's FFI can build a machine-code thunk from that description;
 this port's floor calls OUT to C and has no inbound-callback mechanism at
 all — the door's window procedure is the one callback, and it is written in
 C++ rather than generated.

 THE BOUNDARY: owner-drawn and bitmap menu items do not draw. Ordinary text
 menus — the whole of what DD10's app and DD12's dialogs use — are unaffected,
 because the descriptor is only consulted when an item asks to be drawn by the
 application. The same boundary already applies to
 `MENUITEMINFOW>>commandMenuItem:metrics:`, whose image branch is likewise
 absent, and both wait on the same thing.

 Without this, `Menu class >> initialize` raises and its MESSAGE MAPPINGS are
 never registered — WM_CONTEXTMENU, WM_INITMENU, WM_INITMENUPOPUP — so a menu
 would never be told it is about to be shown. That is a much bigger loss than
 owner-drawing, and it is why this exists rather than letting the initialize
 fail.

 RETIREMENT: an inbound-callback mechanism in the FFI floor. Recorded in
 docs/LOOSE_ENDS.md."
Object subclass: FunctionDescriptor [
    | returnType argumentTypes |
    FunctionDescriptor class >> returnType: aReturnType argumentTypes: aTypeString [
        ^self new setReturnType: aReturnType argumentTypes: aTypeString
    ]
    setReturnType: r argumentTypes: a [ returnType := r. argumentTypes := a ]
    returnType [ ^returnType ]
    argumentTypes [ ^argumentTypes ]
    "A descriptor that can never be called back through answers no thunk.
     Answering 0 rather than nil so a caller passing it to Win32 as a word
     marshals cleanly — Windows treats a null callback as 'no owner draw'."
    asParameter [ ^0 ]
]

"`keyAtValue:` / `keyAtValue:ifAbsent:` — the REVERSE lookup, and the command
 registry depends on it.

 `CommandDescription class >> withId: anInteger ifAbsent:` answers the command
 whose registered id is `anInteger`, which is how Dolphin's
 `wmCommand:wParam:lParam:` turns the id Windows sends back into a real
 command object. Without it every menu command raised inside the contained
 handler-error path.

 `TextEdit>>alignment` uses the same pair against its AlignmentMap.

 Linear, because a Dictionary is indexed by KEY: Dolphin's is linear too."
Object subclass: Dictionary [
    keyAtValue: aValue ifAbsent: aBlock [
        self keysAndValuesDo: [ :k :v | v = aValue ifTrue: [ ^k ] ].
        ^aBlock value
    ]
    keyAtValue: aValue [ ^self keyAtValue: aValue ifAbsent: [ nil ] ]
    "Identity twin, for the registries keyed by object identity."
    keyAtIdentityValue: aValue ifAbsent: aBlock [
        self keysAndValuesDo: [ :k :v | v == aValue ifTrue: [ ^k ] ].
        ^aBlock value
    ]
]

"`addClassVariable:value:` with a COMPUTED name — the one case the translator
 cannot rewrite.

 `self addClassVariable: 'Default' value: x` becomes `Default := x` at
 translation time, because the name is a literal. But
 `Graphics.Color class >> initialize` ends

     #(#black #white) do: [:each |
         self addClassVariable: each capitalized value: (self named: each)]

 where the name is computed. This dialect has no dynamic class-variable
 store, so that assignment cannot happen.

 THE COST: `Color Black` and `Color White` answer nil. `Color black` and
 `Color white` — the lowercase accessors that go through `named:` — work
 normally, and nothing in the port reads the capitalised class variables.
 Answering self keeps `initialize` running to completion, which matters: it
 is the same method that installs `Default`, and an exception here would
 leave THAT unset too.

 RETIREMENT: a dynamic class-variable accessor in the front-end — the same
 one `Behavior>>bindingFor:` waits on."
nil subclass: Object [
    Object class >> addClassVariable: aName value: aValue [ ^aValue ]

    "`addClassConstant:value:` is the same family, and here the no-op answer
     is NOT good enough — these values are read back.

     `DpiAwareness class >> initialize` builds the five awareness levels and
     files each one under its own name:

         inst := self new handle: each value.
         self addClassConstant: each key value: inst.

     and `DpiAwareness perMonitor`/`system`/`unaware`/`gdiScaled` are simply
     `^PerMonitor` and friends. With the selector absent, `initialize` raised
     at the first element, `Awarenesses` never got assigned, and
     `fromHandle:` answered `lookup:` sent to nil — the failure that stopped
     `DisplayMonitor>>cacheInfo` dead and, through it, every window-positioning
     path including `showModal`.

     The translator already emits a `classVar<Name>:` setter for every class
     variable it sees, so the dynamic assignment Dolphin does with a
     metaprogramming call is reachable here as an ordinary perform. Contained,
     because a class constant with no generated setter is a translator gap
     rather than a reason to abort a class's whole initializer — which is the
     specific way this bug did its damage."
    "`subclassResponsibility` ON THE CLASS SIDE. `Object>>subclassResponsibility`
     exists instance-side (st/world/01_object.mst), but Dolphin marks
     CLASS-side methods abstract with it too — `STxFiler class >> on:` and
     `stxFormatName` are both `^self subclassResponsibility`.

     Missing, the abstract itself failed with `class 'STLInFiler' has no
     class-side method 'subclassResponsibility'`, which names the WRONG
     method: the bug is whichever abstract was reached, and that name is the
     one hidden. An abstract that cannot report itself is worse than useless,
     so this exists to make the real culprit say its own name."
    Object class >> subclassResponsibility [
        ^self error: 'subclass responsibility'
    ]

    Object class >> addClassConstant: aName value: aValue [
        [ self perform: ('classVar' , aName , ':') asSymbol
               withArguments: (Array with: aValue) ]
            on: Error do: [ :e | ].
        ^aValue
    ]

    "The two STx filer entry points every CLASS must answer. Dolphin declares
     them on `Core.ClassDescription` — instance methods there, so they are
     available on every class object — as loose methods in `Dolphin STx Filer
     Core.pax`, which is why no .cls carries them and the translated wave has
     neither. Here the shadow chain ends at `Object class`, so that is the
     equivalent home.

     The filers send the TWO-argument form (`class stbReadFrom: decorator
     format: (converters lookup: class)`, STLInFiler>>readObjectWithPrefix:
     and STBInFiler alike); D8 classes override the THREE-argument one. This
     pair is the bridge: read the size, then forward. Without it every object
     in a view resource dies as `Class '_Type' has no instance method
     'stbReadFrom_format_'` the moment the filer reaches its first proxy."
    Object class >> stbReadFrom: anSTxInFiler format: anSTxClassConversion [
        ^self
            stbReadFrom: anSTxInFiler
            format: anSTxClassConversion
            size: (anSTxInFiler readSizeOf: self)
    ]

    Object class >> stbReadFrom: anSTxInFiler format: anSTxClassConversion size: anInteger [
        ^anSTxInFiler
            readObjectOfClass: self
            format: anSTxClassConversion
            size: anInteger
    ]

    "Does the receiver's instances have INDEXED slots? The filers branch on it
     to decide whether a size word is present in the stream — `readSizeOf:` is
     `aClass isVariable ifTrue: [stream next] ifFalse: [0]` — so a wrong answer
     does not raise, it DESYNCHRONISES the parse and every later object is
     garbage. Default false; the variable classes override below.

     This describes DOLPHIN's instance shape, which is what the stream encodes,
     NOT ours. `OrderedCollection isVariable` is true in Dolphin (oracle) and
     stays true here even though our OrderedCollection holds a named `array`
     ivar instead of indexed slots — the size word is in the bytes either way."
    Object class >> isVariable [
        ^false
    ]

    "Are the receiver's instances POINTER objects (as against raw bytes)? The
     filer branches on it to choose `instVarAt:put:` over `basicAt:put:`."
    Object class >> isPointers [
        ^true
    ]

    "The named instance-variable count, from the VM's own field walk — the same
     one `instVarAt:` indexes, so the two cannot disagree. `instSizeOf:` takes
     the class as an argument because a class-side <stprim:> passes only its
     declared parameters, never the receiver."
    Object class >> instSizeOf: aClass [
        <stprim: stInstSize>
    ]

    Object class >> instSize [
        ^self instSizeOf: self
    ]

    "Dolphin's `Behavior>>instanceSpec`, SYNTHESISED. The real one is an image
     bitfield; the filers read exactly two fields out of it —
     `instSpec bitAnd: 255` (the named-ivar count) and `instSpec anyMask: 8192`
     (pointers) — so those two are what this reproduces, and the other bits are
     deliberately absent rather than guessed. Checked against the oracle:
     STBViewProxy 8200 (instSize 8, pointers), ContainerView 8207 (15,
     pointers), String 20992 (bytes)."
    Object class >> instanceSpec [
        ^self instSize + (self isPointers ifTrue: [ 8192 ] ifFalse: [ 0 ])
    ]

    "Allocate with n INDEXED slots. `Object>>basicNew:` in 01_object.mst is a
     bare `<primitive: 24>`, and a numbered primitive is not a dispatch
     mechanism in this front-end (HOUSE_PRIMS.md, the DD8 correction) — the
     body compiles to nothing and ANSWERS SELF, so `aClass basicNew: 0` handed
     the filer the class itself and `instVarAt:put:` then failed with 'index
     out of range', naming the symptom three layers from the cause.

     A plain ST class has no indexed part, so n is ignored here; the variable
     classes override below. `readObjectOfClass:size:` calls this for EVERY
     object it deserialises."
    Object class >> basicNew: n [
        ^self basicNew
    ]

    "The STx format version of the receiver's instances. Dolphin's default is
     0 and the translated wave carries its own overrides (STBViewProxy answers
     11); this is the floor the BRIDGED classes need, since none of them has a
     translated class side. String and Symbol answer 1 (oracle)."
    Object class >> stbVersion [
        ^0
    ]
]

"WHICH CLASSES ARE VARIABLE — every one of these answers came from the oracle,
 asked of the classes the translated resources actually name plus the rest of
 the collection family. NOT guessed, because a wrong answer here does not
 raise: `readSizeOf:` reads a size word only for a variable class, so a class
 wrongly marked fixed leaves that word in the stream and EVERY later object is
 read from the wrong offset. That is exactly how the first attempt failed —
 `Core.IdentityDictionary` was missing from the list, its size word (a 0) was
 then read as an object-reference prefix, and the filer reported 'object 0
 referenced but not yet defined' 26 elements downstream of the real cause.

 Dolphin says variable: Array, OrderedCollection, SortedCollection, Set,
 IdentitySet, Dictionary, LookupTable, IdentityDictionary, Symbol, ByteArray,
 String (and AnsiString/Utf16String).
 Dolphin says fixed: Bag, Association, Message, MessageSend, MessageSequence,
 Semaphore, ScaledDecimal — so they are deliberately absent, not overlooked."
Array class extend [ isVariable [ ^true ] basicNew: n [ ^Array new: n ] ]
Symbol class extend [ isVariable [ ^true ] stbVersion [ ^1 ] ]
OrderedCollection class extend [
    isVariable [ ^true ]
    basicNew: n [ ^OrderedCollection new ]
]
SortedCollection class extend [
    isVariable [ ^true ]
    basicNew: n [ ^SortedCollection new ]
]
Set class extend [ isVariable [ ^true ] basicNew: n [ ^Set new ] ]
IdentitySet class extend [ isVariable [ ^true ] basicNew: n [ ^IdentitySet new ] ]
Dictionary class extend [ isVariable [ ^true ] basicNew: n [ ^Dictionary new ] ]
LookupTable class extend [ isVariable [ ^true ] basicNew: n [ ^LookupTable new ] ]
IdentityDictionary class extend [
    isVariable [ ^true ]
    basicNew: n [ ^IdentityDictionary new ]
]
String class extend [
    isVariable [ ^true ]
    isPointers [ ^false ]
    basicNew: n [ ^String new: n ]
    stbVersion [ ^1 ]
]
ByteArray class extend [
    isVariable [ ^true ]
    isPointers [ ^false ]
    basicNew: n [ ^ByteArray new: n ]
]

"The indexed-slot accessors the filer uses for BYTE objects (`newObject
 basicAt: i put: stream next`). In this port an indexed slot IS the bridged
 collection's element, so these are at:/at:put: — kept as separate selectors
 because Dolphin's own code distinguishes them and a subclass may override
 at:put: without meaning to change the raw store."
Object extend [
    basicAt: i [ ^self at: i ]
    basicAt: i put: v [ ^self at: i put: v ]
    basicSize [ ^self size ]
]

"`canUnderstand:` — Dolphin's 'do instances of me understand this selector'.

 One caller in the translated wave: `Color>>printNamed:on:` asks
 `Color class canUnderstand: aSymbol` before printing a colour by its name,
 so an unnamed colour prints as its components instead.

 Implemented as `respondsTo:` on the same receiver. That is NOT the general
 definition — Dolphin's asks about INSTANCES of the receiver, and this asks
 about the receiver itself — and the difference is recorded in
 `docs/LOOSE_ENDS.md` beside `conformsToProtocol:`, which wants the general
 version and cannot have it for the same reason. For the metaclass receiver
 the one caller uses, the two coincide."
nil subclass: Object [
    canUnderstand: aSymbol [ ^self respondsTo: aSymbol ]
]

"`asSortedArray:` / `asArrayCopy` — Dolphin's one-off sort.

     asSortedArray: aDyadicValuable
         ^self asArrayCopy sort: aDyadicValuable

 `Color class >> initializeCommonColors` builds its lookup by sorting the
 colour table. Distinct from `asSortedCollection:`, which keeps the sort block
 and re-sorts on every add — this is a snapshot, and Dolphin uses it where the
 order is wanted once."
Object subclass: Collection [
    asArrayCopy [ ^Array withAll: self ]
    asSortedArray: aDyadicValuable [
        ^(self asSortedCollection: aDyadicValuable) asArray
    ]
]

"`lookup:` on a DICTIONARY — rescued from the deleted `06_winview.mst`.

 It lived in that file only because `WinView` was written at the same time as
 the `LayoutContext` work that needed it. It has nothing to do with the
 adapter, and deleting the adapter took it with it: `LayoutContext` asks its
 placement table `(placements lookup: aView)` on every layout pass, so the
 whole BorderLayout arrangement silently reverted to the 100x100 creation
 default — every view laid out, none at the size it asked for.

 A REMINDER, and the reason this comment is long: when a scaffolding file is
 deleted, what it happened to HOST is not the same as what it was FOR. The
 gates caught this immediately, which is the only reason it is a footnote."
Object subclass: Dictionary [
    lookup: aKey [ ^self at: aKey ifAbsent: [ nil ] ]
]
Object subclass: IdentityDictionary [
    lookup: aKey [ ^self at: aKey ifAbsent: [ nil ] ]
]

"`Boolean>>asParameter` — Dolphin's own, and the mirror of the `#b` return.

 `TextEdit>>isTextModified:` is `self sendMessage: EM_SETMODIFY wParam:
 aBoolean asParameter`, and `Object>>asParameter` answers self — so a Boolean
 reached the FFI floor as a Boolean and was refused.

 The floor now also accepts true/false directly as 1/0, for the same reason it
 accepts nil as NULL: `#b` means a BOOL-returning call ANSWERS a Boolean, so
 Booleans are values the floor itself produces and must take back. This
 method is the narrower, Dolphin-faithful half of that — it puts the
 conversion where Dolphin puts it, at the source."
Object subclass: Boolean [
    asParameter [ ^self ifTrue: [ 1 ] ifFalse: [ 0 ] ]
]

"`Exception>>beep` — the audible half of Dolphin's bad-input policy.

 `ValueConvertingControlView>>updateModel` catches `InvalidFormat` and does
 `exception beep` before reverting the field. This is the DD10 brief's
 'beep-and-revert', and the beep is not decoration: it is the only feedback a
 user gets that the value they typed was rejected.

 `Sound errorBeep` is MessageBeep(MB_ICONERROR), which respects the user's
 sound scheme — including silence, if that is what they chose."
Error subclass: Exception [
    beep [ ^Sound errorBeep ]
]

"`String>>#<<` — Dolphin's message-argument interpolation, the spelling every
 STx error uses: `STBError signal: 'Unsupported <2s> format (version <1d>)'
 << { anInteger. self stxFormatName }`.

 Without it EVERY filer error raised `Class 'String' has no instance method
 '<<'` INSTEAD of the message it was trying to report — so a legitimate,
 well-worded diagnostic ('input stream is inconsistent (object 2 referenced
 but not yet defined)') surfaced as a missing-selector error naming neither
 the problem nor the stream. The formatter is on the diagnosis path, so it is
 worth more than its size.

 `<Ns>` and `<Nd>` insert the displayString of argument N, `<Np>` its
 printString (oracle: `'<1p>' << #('abc')` answers `'abc'` WITH quotes, and
 `'<2s>-<1s>' << #('a' 'b')` answers 'b-a' — the index selects, so arguments
 may be used out of order or not at all). A `<` that does not begin a
 well-formed field is copied through unchanged."
String extend [
    << anArray [
        | out i size |
        out := WriteStream on: String new.
        i := 1.
        size := self size.
        [ i <= size ] whileTrue: [
            | ch |
            ch := self at: i.
            (ch = $< and: [ i < size and: [ (self at: i + 1) isDigit ] ])
                ifTrue: [
                    | j num kind |
                    j := i + 1.
                    num := 0.
                    [ j <= size and: [ (self at: j) isDigit ] ] whileTrue: [
                        num := num * 10 + (self at: j) digitValue.
                        j := j + 1 ].
                    kind := j <= size ifTrue: [ self at: j ] ifFalse: [ nil ].
                    (kind notNil and: [ (j + 1) <= size
                                        and: [ (self at: j + 1) = $> ] ])
                        ifTrue: [
                            | arg |
                            arg := (num >= 1 and: [ num <= anArray size ])
                                ifTrue: [ anArray at: num ]
                                ifFalse: [ nil ].
                            kind = $p
                                ifTrue: [ out nextPutAll: arg printString ]
                                ifFalse: [ out nextPutAll: arg displayString ].
                            i := j + 2 ]
                        ifFalse: [ out nextPut: ch. i := i + 1 ] ]
                ifFalse: [ out nextPut: ch. i := i + 1 ] ].
        ^out contents
    ]
]

"── Routing the COLLECTION filer overrides onto OUR hierarchy (DD16) ────
 `Dolphin STx Filer Core.pax` files `stbReadFrom:format:size:` onto
 `Core.LookupTable`, `Core.OrderedCollection` and `Core.Set`, and the
 translator now emits all three (`st/mvp/90_*_loose.mst`). Two of them land
 where they are needed. LookupTable's does NOT, because the hierarchies
 differ:

     Dolphin   IdentityDictionary -> LookupTable -> Dictionary -> Set
     ours      IdentityDictionary -> Dictionary  -> HashedCollection

 so nothing in our chain reaches `LookupTable class`. Dictionary is where our
 chain converges, so the same delegation is declared here — pointing at the
 SAME `readLookupTable:format:size:` the translated override calls, not a
 reimplementation of it.

 This matters because a dictionary cannot survive the generic raw-slot path
 at all: Dolphin's has 2 named ivars with the hash buckets in its indexed
 slots, ours has 3 and a different internal structure. `readLookupTable:`
 reads KEYS AND VALUES and builds the collection through `at:put:`, which is
 representation-independent — the only way this can be right."
Dictionary class extend [
    stbReadFrom: anSTxInFiler format: aFormat size: anInteger [
        ^anSTxInFiler readLookupTable: self format: aFormat size: anInteger
    ]
]

"── `become:` — the PROXY mechanism, exposed at last (DD16) ─────────────
 `STxProxy>>stbFixup:at:` is `^self become: self value`: a deserialised proxy
 REPLACES ITSELF with the object it stands for, so every reference the filer
 has already handed out — readMap entries, ivars of objects read earlier —
 points at the real thing without any of them being revisited. That is the
 whole reason STB/STL can carry forward references.

 The VM has had the primitive since Sprint 9 (`stBecome`, a two-way swap via
 shallow copies) but nothing ever named it in Smalltalk, so the corpus's own
 spelling raised doesNotUnderstand at the last step of a successful read.

 Instance-side `<stprim:>` passes the receiver, unlike the class side."
Object extend [
    become: anObject [ <stprim: stBecome> ]
    becomeForward: anObject [ <stprim: stBecomeForward> ]
]
